WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.895
Welcome to Module 1 of the
Privileged Access learning path:

2
00:00:03.895 --> 00:00:06.563
inventory every privileged assignment.

3
00:00:06.563 --> 00:00:10.500
Privilege should be just-in-time,
by tier, and provable.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:15.037
This path teaches you to build the software
that gets an estate there, in eight

5
00:00:15.037 --> 00:00:20.000
modules, one for each chapter of the developer
workbook, Building Privileged Access.

6
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.080
This one serves Phase
0 of the project plan,

7
00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:29.675
tasks A0.1 and A0.2, and Gate 0,
with Chapter 01 of the workbook.

8
00:00:29.675 --> 00:00:33.565
In it you run Microsoft Learn's
own samples to see what they miss,

9
00:00:33.565 --> 00:00:38.204
then run the workbook's inventory: one
read of every privileged assignment,

10
00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:41.496
a tier for every row, findings
for standing privilege,

11
00:00:41.496 --> 00:00:44.937
and a manifest that says which
calls produced the files.

12
00:00:44.937 --> 00:00:48.175
The code runs on your laptop
against a fictional tenant.

13
00:00:48.175 --> 00:00:53.650
It's illustrative: tested against fixtures,
not run against a production tenant.

14
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.438
Why start here?

15
00:00:58.438 --> 00:01:02.702
Because you can't make privilege
just-in-time until you can list it:

16
00:01:02.702 --> 00:01:06.415
who holds which role, at which
scope, standing or eligible,

17
00:01:06.415 --> 00:01:09.337
directly or through a
group, and in which tier.

18
00:01:09.337 --> 00:01:12.751
The admin center's list of
active assignments isn't enough.

19
00:01:12.751 --> 00:01:16.070
Under PIM almost every human
holder becomes eligible,

20
00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:19.979
so a list of active assignments
shrinks as the project succeeds,

21
00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:23.225
and it stops being an inventory
just when you need one.

22
00:01:23.225 --> 00:01:27.500
In this module you build the inventory
the rest of the path depends on:

23
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.075
one read of every privileged assignment,
a tier for every row, findings for

24
00:01:32.075 --> 00:01:37.150
standing privilege, and a manifest that
says which calls produced the files.

25
00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.711
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

26
00:01:43.711 --> 00:01:46.075
which takes about fifty
minutes with the lab.

27
00:01:46.075 --> 00:01:49.041
You'll name the four ways
a role reaches a person,

28
00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:54.137
and explain why the inventory reads eligible
assignments as well as active ones.

29
00:01:54.137 --> 00:01:56.508
You'll explain what
Microsoft Learn's sample

30
00:01:56.508 --> 00:01:58.687
for listing role assignments leaves out.

31
00:01:58.687 --> 00:02:04.499
You'll run the inventory and read its
findings, rules I N V oh one to oh nine.

32
00:02:04.499 --> 00:02:08.475
You'll explain why the tier comes
from the program, not from Microsoft.

33
00:02:08.475 --> 00:02:11.225
And you'll say what the
reader refuses, and why.

34
00:02:11.225 --> 00:02:14.564
Before you start, you need
the path's prerequisites:

35
00:02:14.564 --> 00:02:18.301
the chapter on privileged
tiers in Access and Privilege,

36
00:02:18.301 --> 00:02:22.913
the plan's gates, Microsoft Automation
Modules 1 and 2, and PowerShell 7.

37
00:02:22.913 --> 00:02:26.569
Lab 1 needs PowerShell
7.1 or later with Pester

38
00:02:26.569 --> 00:02:29.937
5 and a copy of the
repository, and no tenant.

39
00:02:29.937 --> 00:02:32.838
Lab 2 needs a worldwide
or G C C test tenant

40
00:02:32.838 --> 00:02:36.690
with a test Azure
subscription, never production.

41
00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:43.152
Unit 1: what the inventory has to hold.

42
00:02:43.152 --> 00:02:48.160
Microsoft Learn's guide to planning a PIM
deployment names four kinds of assignment:

43
00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.953
permanent eligible, permanent active,
time-bound eligible and time-bound active.

44
00:02:52.953 --> 00:02:56.514
And it says to keep zero
permanently active assignments,

45
00:02:56.514 --> 00:02:58.939
except the emergency access accounts.

46
00:02:58.939 --> 00:03:03.502
So the inventory reads all four,
eligible as well as active.

47
00:03:03.502 --> 00:03:04.803
Next, the route.

48
00:03:04.803 --> 00:03:09.266
A role assigned to a role-assignable
group reaches every member of that group.

49
00:03:09.266 --> 00:03:13.296
The program's design assigns
roles to groups named P R V,

50
00:03:13.296 --> 00:03:16.790
then the tier, then the
role, and never to a person.

51
00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:17.815
Then the tier.

52
00:03:17.815 --> 00:03:21.447
It comes from the program's
own file, tiers dot json,

53
00:03:21.447 --> 00:03:26.053
keyed by the role's template I D,
which is the same in every tenant.

54
00:03:26.053 --> 00:03:31.178
A role that isn't in the file is reported
as unclassified, never assumed safe.

55
00:03:31.178 --> 00:03:33.988
That file is this
workbook's design guidance:

56
00:03:33.988 --> 00:03:37.620
the agency replaces it with
its approved tier model.

57
00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:46.061
Microsoft Learn's page on listing Microsoft
Entra role assignments has a sample,

58
00:03:46.061 --> 00:03:48.932
and the kit runs it as
published against a stand-in,

59
00:03:48.932 --> 00:03:51.032
so you can see exactly what it misses.

60
00:03:51.032 --> 00:03:54.471
The most important gap: it
sees no eligible assignment.

61
00:03:54.471 --> 00:03:58.272
That's the state the project
moves every human holder into,

62
00:03:58.272 --> 00:04:02.233
so an inventory built on it gets
emptier as the work succeeds.

63
00:04:02.233 --> 00:04:04.927
It also returns a role-assignable group as

64
00:04:04.927 --> 00:04:07.621
one principal and never reads its members.

65
00:04:07.621 --> 00:04:10.558
It resolves no names and assigns no tier.

66
00:04:10.558 --> 00:04:16.033
It passes neither the All nor the page size
option, so it doesn't follow every page.

67
00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:20.782
It reads transitive assignments from
the beta endpoint, on a hard-coded host.

68
00:04:20.782 --> 00:04:23.332
And it reads no Azure
role assignments at all.

69
00:04:23.332 --> 00:04:26.810
The workbook's inventory
closes each of those gaps,

70
00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:31.400
and Lab 1's first step shows the
eligible gap with your own eyes.

71
00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:39.137
Unit 2: the reader, the
rows and the findings.

72
00:04:39.137 --> 00:04:42.654
The reader sends only the calls
declared in the kit's call list,

73
00:04:42.654 --> 00:04:45.137
each with its least-privileged permission.

74
00:04:45.137 --> 00:04:48.099
It sends GET only; any
other method throws.

75
00:04:48.099 --> 00:04:51.499
It sends to one host, the
named cloud's own Graph or

76
00:04:51.499 --> 00:04:55.238
Resource Manager endpoint,
and it follows every page.

77
00:04:55.238 --> 00:04:58.169
Note why it asks for User
dot Read Basic dot All

78
00:04:58.169 --> 00:05:01.024
as well as Group Member
dot Read Basic dot All.

79
00:05:01.024 --> 00:05:05.538
Without it, a group's member comes
back with an I D and a type only,

80
00:05:05.538 --> 00:05:09.875
and the review sees an unnamed
holder; that's rule I N V oh nine.

81
00:05:09.875 --> 00:05:13.963
The rows: one row per
assignment, with its plane, role,

82
00:05:13.963 --> 00:05:19.025
tier and scope, its state, and its
route, direct or through a group.

83
00:05:19.025 --> 00:05:23.199
When a role is on a group, the reader
reads the group's members once,

84
00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:26.710
and each member gets its
own row marked Via Group.

85
00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:33.485
The findings, I N V oh one to oh nine.

86
00:05:33.485 --> 00:05:36.869
Oh one is the big one:
standing active privilege in

87
00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:40.335
Tier 0 or Tier 1 outside
the documented exceptions.

88
00:05:40.335 --> 00:05:44.622
An exception counts only if it has
an owner, and then the row is Info.

89
00:05:44.622 --> 00:05:49.373
Oh two is a role assigned straight to
a user, not through a P R V group.

90
00:05:49.373 --> 00:05:53.285
Oh three is a group that holds a
role but isn't named for its tier.

91
00:05:53.285 --> 00:05:57.787
Oh four counts Global Administrators:
five or more is a finding,

92
00:05:57.787 --> 00:06:03.588
because Microsoft Learn's best practices
for Entra roles advise fewer than five,

93
00:06:03.588 --> 00:06:08.524
and so is fewer than two users, the
floor in CISA's ScubaGear baseline,

94
00:06:08.524 --> 00:06:10.948
M S A A D seven point one, version one.

95
00:06:10.948 --> 00:06:13.010
Oh five is a role with no tier.

96
00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:16.010
Oh six is eligibility that never expires.

97
00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:19.360
Oh seven is User Access
Administrator at root scope,

98
00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:21.698
which is what elevate access grants.

99
00:06:21.698 --> 00:06:25.157
Oh eight is more than three
Owners on a subscription,

100
00:06:25.157 --> 00:06:29.661
against Microsoft Learn's Azure R B
A C best practice of at most three.

101
00:06:29.661 --> 00:06:33.560
And oh nine is a group member
whose name the run couldn't read.

102
00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.811
Those thresholds come from
Microsoft Learn and CISA;

103
00:06:36.811 --> 00:06:39.490
the other rules are the kit's own design.

104
00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:43.965
Try it.

105
00:06:43.965 --> 00:06:48.036
Lab 1 runs in the privileged-access
folder of the examples,

106
00:06:48.036 --> 00:06:53.040
in one PowerShell 7 session, against
a fictional tenant served by a fake.

107
00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:55.940
Nothing signs in, and
nothing leaves the machine.

108
00:06:55.940 --> 00:07:00.865
Step one runs Microsoft Learn's samples
against the stand-ins, with Pester.

109
00:07:00.865 --> 00:07:03.992
Nine pass, none fail, and seven don't run:

110
00:07:03.992 --> 00:07:07.927
those seven are Chapter
02's, for the next module.

111
00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:10.390
The second test is the one to notice.

112
00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:12.891
It shows that the fixture's eligible-only

113
00:07:12.891 --> 00:07:15.540
holders never appear in
the sample's output.

114
00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.928
Step two loads the module and lists
the permissions the inventory needs.

115
00:07:19.928 --> 00:07:24.765
There are five: Group Member, Role
Assignment Schedule, Role Eligibility

116
00:07:24.765 --> 00:07:29.602
Schedule and Role Management, each to
read, and User dot Read Basic dot All.

117
00:07:29.602 --> 00:07:31.278
Nothing ends in ReadWrite.

118
00:07:31.278 --> 00:07:34.320
The output on the slide is wrapped to fit.

119
00:07:38.070 --> 00:07:41.882
Step three runs the inventory
against the fictional tenant.

120
00:07:41.882 --> 00:07:46.703
You give it the fixture, an output folder,
three Azure scopes, two subscriptions and

121
00:07:46.703 --> 00:07:51.595
the root management group, the exceptions
list, and the control-plane subscription.

122
00:07:51.595 --> 00:07:53.445
It reads thirty rows.

123
00:07:53.445 --> 00:07:57.895
In Azure, four are Tier 0,
five Tier 1 and one Tier 2.

124
00:07:57.895 --> 00:08:03.413
In Entra, eight are Tier 0, two Tier 1,
two Tier 2, and one is unclassified:

125
00:08:03.413 --> 00:08:08.833
a role that isn't in the tier file,
reported rather than assumed safe.

126
00:08:08.833 --> 00:08:13.595
The run raises ten findings, seven
warnings and one information item.

127
00:08:13.595 --> 00:08:16.296
And it writes everything
to the output folder,

128
00:08:16.296 --> 00:08:20.631
with a manifest that lists every request
it sent and the hash of every file

129
00:08:20.631 --> 00:08:24.895
it wrote, so a reviewer can check later
which calls produced the evidence.

130
00:08:24.895 --> 00:08:28.100
The scope list on the
slide is wrapped to fit.

131
00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:34.212
Step four prints the ten findings.

132
00:08:34.212 --> 00:08:39.423
Seven are I N V oh one, standing
privilege in Tier 0 or Tier 1 outside the

133
00:08:39.423 --> 00:08:41.891
exceptions: a person, alex dot kim,

134
00:08:41.891 --> 00:08:45.638
an automation account,
and several Azure holders.

135
00:08:45.638 --> 00:08:52.013
The Azure rows show object I D's, because
Resource Manager returns I D's, not names.

136
00:08:52.013 --> 00:08:56.939
One holder also has I N V oh seven, User
Access Administrator at root scope,

137
00:08:56.939 --> 00:09:02.112
the elevated access that reaches every
subscription and management group.

138
00:09:02.112 --> 00:09:05.800
There's I N V oh four, too
many Global Administrators,

139
00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:09.575
and I N V oh eight, too many
Owners on one subscription.

140
00:09:09.575 --> 00:09:13.463
Notice who's missing: the
emergency account, E A oh one.

141
00:09:13.463 --> 00:09:17.395
It's Info, not a finding, because
the exceptions list names it,

142
00:09:17.395 --> 00:09:19.401
and the exception has an owner.

143
00:09:19.401 --> 00:09:22.160
An exception with no owner wouldn't count.

144
00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:28.222
Step five shows the reader refusing.

145
00:09:28.222 --> 00:09:31.438
You build a reader for the
inventory task in G C C,

146
00:09:31.438 --> 00:09:35.397
on the fake, and ask it for the
Conditional Access policies.

147
00:09:35.397 --> 00:09:38.775
That call isn't declared for the
inventory task, so it throws,

148
00:09:38.775 --> 00:09:42.085
and tells you to add it to the
call list with its permission.

149
00:09:42.085 --> 00:09:46.723
Then you ask for a cloud called
G C C High, with capital letters.

150
00:09:46.723 --> 00:09:52.644
The names are case-sensitive on
purpose, so that's an unknown cloud,

151
00:09:52.644 --> 00:09:56.811
and it throws too: use global,
G C C, G C C High or D O D.

152
00:09:56.811 --> 00:09:58.322
There's no default cloud.

153
00:09:58.322 --> 00:10:03.522
Step six runs the whole test suite,
three hundred thirty tests, and all pass.

154
00:10:03.522 --> 00:10:08.085
Then delete the output folder, because it
names the fixture's privileged holders.

155
00:10:08.085 --> 00:10:13.030
Pester prints its summary on several
lines; the slide shows it on one.

156
00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:21.944
Lab 2 runs the inventory against your own
test tenant, in the worldwide or G C C

157
00:10:21.944 --> 00:10:27.518
cloud, with a test Azure subscription,
never production, and with fictional people.

158
00:10:27.518 --> 00:10:29.255
The run only reads.

159
00:10:29.255 --> 00:10:31.822
You register a reader
app with a certificate,

160
00:10:31.822 --> 00:10:35.638
and grant it the five Graph
application permissions from step two,

161
00:10:35.638 --> 00:10:38.830
with admin consent, and Reader
on the test subscription.

162
00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:43.186
Then you run the inventory, cross-check
it against the admin center's download,

163
00:10:43.186 --> 00:10:45.705
and check the two things
the fixture assumed.

164
00:10:45.705 --> 00:10:49.518
To back it out, remove the Reader
assignment, the five permissions,

165
00:10:49.518 --> 00:10:53.967
the app registration and its certificate,
and delete the test output folder,

166
00:10:53.967 --> 00:10:56.580
which names the tenant's
privileged holders.

167
00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:02.530
The path's authors haven't run Lab 2;
the workbook's Lab 2 gives every step.

168
00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:07.667
The federal requirement.

169
00:11:07.667 --> 00:11:10.733
The inventory is the evidence
for the plan's Gate 0:

170
00:11:10.733 --> 00:11:15.293
standing administrative privilege
inventoried and classified by tier.

171
00:11:15.293 --> 00:11:19.999
It supports NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three A C two,

172
00:11:19.999 --> 00:11:23.191
account management, and A
C six, enhancement five,

173
00:11:23.191 --> 00:11:26.542
privileged accounts
restricted to defined roles.

174
00:11:26.542 --> 00:11:32.207
And it supports CISA's B O D twenty-five
oh-one and the ScubaGear baseline M S A A D

175
00:11:32.207 --> 00:11:37.437
seven point four, version one: no
permanent active assignment to a highly

176
00:11:37.437 --> 00:11:42.492
privileged role, with the emergency
access accounts as the exception.

177
00:11:42.492 --> 00:11:46.268
The table pairs each with
a KSI and an evidence file.

178
00:11:46.268 --> 00:11:51.553
KSIs are FedRAMP's twenty X Key Security
Indicators for cloud service providers;

179
00:11:51.553 --> 00:11:55.734
the agency lines its evidence up
with what its providers report,

180
00:11:55.734 --> 00:11:57.706
and doesn't report KSIs itself.

181
00:11:57.706 --> 00:12:01.797
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
Gate 0: the inventory files,

182
00:12:01.797 --> 00:12:06.587
in the access-controlled evidence
store, because they name people;

183
00:12:06.587 --> 00:12:10.950
the findings; and the manifest,
beside the files it lists.

184
00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:16.675
Check yourself with two questions.

185
00:12:16.675 --> 00:12:21.813
First: why doesn't the inventory use Microsoft
Learn's all-roles sample as its read?

186
00:12:21.813 --> 00:12:25.410
The answer is b: the sample
sees no eligible assignment,

187
00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:29.487
and that's the state the project
moves every human holder into.

188
00:12:29.487 --> 00:12:32.861
Under PIM almost every
human holder is eligible,

189
00:12:32.861 --> 00:12:36.150
so an active-only list
stops being an inventory.

190
00:12:36.150 --> 00:12:40.021
Second: why is the emergency
account, E A oh one,

191
00:12:40.021 --> 00:12:43.788
reported as Info, not as
an I N V oh one finding?

192
00:12:43.788 --> 00:12:47.562
The answer is c: it's on the
exceptions list with an owner.

193
00:12:47.562 --> 00:12:50.076
An exception with no owner doesn't count.

194
00:12:50.076 --> 00:12:52.450
You saw that in Lab 1, step four.

195
00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:57.720
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the section that answers it.

196
00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:02.295
To sum up.

197
00:13:02.295 --> 00:13:07.183
You ran Microsoft Learn's samples and
saw them miss every eligible holder.

198
00:13:07.183 --> 00:13:11.514
Then you read thirty assignments from
a fictional tenant, each with a tier,

199
00:13:11.514 --> 00:13:13.290
and found standing privilege,

200
00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:17.195
too many Global Administrators
and too many subscription Owners.

201
00:13:17.195 --> 00:13:21.583
And you saw the reader refuse an
undeclared call and an unknown cloud.

202
00:13:21.583 --> 00:13:24.580
Carry one thing forward:
the emergency account

203
00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:27.808
was Info only because the
exceptions list names it.

204
00:13:27.808 --> 00:13:31.419
Next is Module 2: emergency access first.

205
00:13:31.419 --> 00:13:35.973
It's the one step in this project that
can't move, because approval switched on

206
00:13:35.973 --> 00:13:40.670
before the emergency accounts exist is
the sequence that locks the agency out.

207
00:13:40.670 --> 00:13:45.657
In Module 2 you check each emergency
account against Microsoft Learn's standard,

208
00:13:45.657 --> 00:13:48.990
and build the alert that
watches them between drills.
